# Line endings, only where a program actually depends on them.
#
# Deliberately not `* text=auto`. That is the usual recommendation and it would
# renormalise every file in the repository on the next checkout, producing a
# diff nobody asked for in order to fix two files that can say so themselves.
# Three rules, each for a failure that has a name:
#
#   *.cmd / *.bat  cmd.exe is documented to work with either, but `goto` into a
#                  label -- which scripts/panel.cmd is built out of -- is where
#                  LF-only batch files are known to misbehave. A Windows user
#                  with core.autocrlf=input would otherwise check out the one
#                  file whose whole job is to work on Windows in the one form
#                  that might not.
#
#   *.sh           the mirror image, and the more common failure: a Windows
#                  clone with core.autocrlf=true hands scripts/dev.sh to bash
#                  with CRLF, and bash reports `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such
#                  file or directory` -- an error that names neither the file
#                  nor the cause.
#
#   *.svg          docs/assets/ is generated, and the panel inlines the same
#                  bytes it publishes. Two checkouts disagreeing on line endings
#                  would make the same tree build two different pages.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.sh  text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
